---
title: Quick start
description: A command, a query, an event and an interceptor, from an empty project to a running dispatch.
---

This walks the whole surface once. Everything here compiles against the preview packages
described in [Installation](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/installation).

## 1. Register Ergosfare

One call at the composition root. `RegisterGenerated()` is written by the source generator and
names every construct it found:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated()));
```

Register only the modules you use. A project with no events needs no event module.

## 2. A command

A command is an instruction. It has exactly one handler, and it either produces a result or it
does not:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = new Product(command.Name, command.Price);
        await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
        return product.Id;
    }
}
```

The handler is an ordinary class. Its constructor parameters are resolved from the dispatching
scope, so a scoped `IProductRepository` behaves exactly as it does in a controller.

Dispatch it:

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (CreateProduct command, ICommandMediator mediator) =>
{
    var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command);
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});
```

For a command with no result, implement `ICommand` and `ICommandHandler<TCommand>`, and the
handler returns a plain `ValueTask`.

## 3. A query

A query asks a question and changes nothing:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<ProductDto>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public async ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = await repository.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
        return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
```

For a sequence, declare `IStreamQuery<T>` and implement `IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, T>`. Its
handler returns `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` and the caller reads it with `await foreach`.

## 4. An event

An event is something that already happened. Every matching subscriber runs, and none of them
answers the publisher:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name);

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail(IEmailSender sender) : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => sender.SendAsync($"Product {@event.Name} created", context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Notice that `ProductCreated` implements nothing. Writing the subscriber is what makes it an
event, so the project that declares your domain types needs no Ergosfare reference.

```csharp
await mediator.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, name));
```

{% aside type="note" %}
A publish nobody subscribes to is a build error, not a silent no-op. If you write the event and
forget the subscriber, the compiler says so with `ERGO005` rather than letting the call return
having delivered nothing.
{% /aside %}

## 5. An interceptor

Interceptors are how cross-cutting work attaches without the handler knowing. This one runs
before every command:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

It needs no registration of its own — `RegisterGenerated()` already found it. Narrow it to one
message by implementing `ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>`, which also lets you return the
typed message:

```csharp
public sealed class TrimName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

There are four stages — pre, post, exception and final — covered in
[Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors).

## 6. Build it

Build, and read what the compiler tells you. Ergosfare reports what it can prove:

```
error ERGO005: This dispatch of 'ArchiveProduct' can never reach a handler…
warning ERGO007: Handler 'ReindexProduct' handles 'ReindexProduct', but no dispatch site in
the compiled closure can deliver that message — the handler never executes.
```

Neither is a lint suggestion. The first says a call will always fail; the second says code you
wrote will never run. See
[Working with diagnostics](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/diagnostics) for how to read
them, and when suppressing one is the right answer.

